  "date": "2026-10-02",
  "status": "Native Release/Debug, strict documentation and Valgrind qualification passed with only previously approved external origins. Cross-distribution RPM qualification remains pending.",
  "root_causes": {
    "arrays": "The array constructor claimed a borrowed local jclass and deleted it twice. It now retains its own global reference without adopting the local reference.",
    "exception_conversion": "QoreException accessors and UTF extraction used unchecked JNI calls and leaked UTF buffers; checked calls and holders preserve the original exception if conversion fails.",
    "shutdown_handles": "A queue sentinel could overtake queued handles and omitted Java-live wrappers. Shutdown now closes registration, snapshots outstanding handles, joins the worker and atomically claims every remaining pointer.",
    "closed_program": "A dispatcher constructed after Program deletion resurrected its zero strong count without restoring its dependency reference. Releasing the final closure then freed the Program before the dispatcher dereferenced it. A dependency reference preserves metadata while closed execution remains rejected.",
    "destructor_errors": "A C++ destructor attempted to construct a Java exception after native registration had closed, allowing a JavaException through a noexcept destructor. Explicit destruction reports errors; GC/shutdown destruction clears errors with no caller. Failed exception-wrapper allocation clears its owned sink.",
    "callback_result": "Dispatcher::dispatch owned the referenced Qore callback result but did not discard it after Java conversion. An existing string callback leaked 56 direct and 88 indirect bytes; ValueHolder now releases the result on success and exceptions.",
    "docs": "Generated C++ inputs, unqualified references, invalid tags/parameters and malformed tables caused warnings and broken public indexes. Two-phase public indexes and installed dependency tags now pass strict docs and five generated-output checks."
  },
